WEBVTT

00:00.200 --> 00:07.675
Our NeurIPS 2026 paper introduces Linguistic Trajectory Encoding, or LTE,

00:07.915 --> 00:11.158
a compact spatial memory for embodied agents.

00:11.668 --> 00:13.443
Video memories find clips.

00:13.613 --> 00:16.226
Geometric memories locate objects.

00:16.476 --> 00:18.833
But what happened to an object over time?

00:19.193 --> 00:22.035
Where was the dishwashing liquid last used yesterday morning?

00:22.575 --> 00:24.701
We track objects in 3D.

00:24.941 --> 00:27.224
An octree indexes static space.

00:27.414 --> 00:29.696
LTE records moving objects.

00:29.906 --> 00:31.599
Then we answer the query.

00:31.929 --> 00:35.816
The key idea is a language-searchable timeline for each tracked object.

00:36.206 --> 00:42.390
LTE summarizes motion in language, with sparse 3D anchors and visual evidence.

00:42.940 --> 00:45.946
The phone moves from near the dining table to the stairwell.

00:46.236 --> 00:51.077
A category query finds the phone's last observation at 29.5 seconds.

00:51.427 --> 00:56.026
Another clip finds the laptop's last observation at 7.5 seconds.

00:56.526 --> 01:02.478
Across 24 hours of video, trajectory storage shrinks 26.1-fold.

01:02.838 --> 01:08.144
After memory construction, full-system queries take 0.43 seconds.

01:08.594 --> 01:14.076
On SMB, semantic trajectory retrieval reaches 45.3%.

01:14.416 --> 01:17.731
The best VLM reaches 31.9%.

01:18.221 --> 01:24.910
On Ego4D natural-language queries, zero-shot Recall@5 is 55.10%.

01:25.270 --> 01:28.903
VideoAgent reaches 33.05%.

01:29.453 --> 01:32.031
The paper, code, and demos are online.

01:32.451 --> 01:32.974
Let's talk.
